WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.557
TODD PRIVES: Hey, everyone.

00:00:14.557 --> 00:00:16.640
Thanks for coming to this
afternoon session, where

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.310
we'll be talking about
visual effects in animation

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.500
rendering on GCP.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:22.550
My name's Todd Prives.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.767
I'm a product manager
here at Google,

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:27.100
and I focus on solutions for
the media and entertainment

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.470
vertical.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:29.980
So during this
particular session,

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.439
we've got some
Oscar-caliber customers who

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:35.720
are going to share with you
their experience of using GCP

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:41.470
to render imagery that's
high quality, cost effective,

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:43.380
helps them deliver
projects on time,

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.900
and really most importantly
as far as visual effects

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.715
animation goes, it serves the
creative purpose of the story

00:00:50.715 --> 00:00:52.590
So I'll start by giving
you a brief overview,

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.964
talk a little bit
more about what

00:00:53.964 --> 00:00:55.582
rendering is at a high level.

00:00:55.582 --> 00:00:57.290
We'll look at some of
the things that GCP

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.780
has done to enable
studios to achieve really

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.381
great high-scale rendering.

00:01:01.381 --> 00:01:03.130
And then we'll hear
from Monique Bradshaw.

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:04.810
Monique's VP of
business development

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.030
and operations at ConductorIO.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.830
They're a cloud rendering
platform running on GCP,

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:12.340
and their customer,
Atomic Fiction,

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.770
just put through several
million hours of rendering

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:17.292
on GCP on the film "The Walk."

00:01:17.292 --> 00:01:19.500
And this is a film from
Oscar winner Robert Zemeckis,

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.580
and the film itself
was shortlisted

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:25.170
for the Oscar Bake-off for
best visual effects this year.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.250
After Monique, we'll hear
from Brennan Chapman.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.190
He's the head of pipeline
at Moonbot Studios,

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.250
they themselves an
Oscar-winning studio

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.471
in Shreveport, Louisiana.

00:01:32.471 --> 00:01:34.220
And Brennan is going
to go ahead and share

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.590
his experience of using GCP
to set up a hybrid environment

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.340
for rendering, where
he's been able to use

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.600
his on-premise
resources and then burst

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.361
to GCP for excess capacity
and really demanding jobs.

00:01:46.361 --> 00:01:48.666
So at a high level,
what is rendering?

00:01:48.666 --> 00:01:50.540
Well, there are several
different variations,

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.670
but really for the purpose
of media, entertainment,

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.870
and animation, it
comes down to this--

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.660
taking a model inside of
a 3-D application that's

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.490
been built by an
artist, and taking

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.590
all of the data associated
with that model--

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.170
so that could be textures.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.780
That could be geometry.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:06.690
That could be assets.

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:07.580
That could be animation.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:08.538
That could be lighting.

00:02:08.538 --> 00:02:10.350
Imagine to accurately
light this,

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.060
you could need to set up
thousands of points of light.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.450
So it's taking all of this
data and number crunching

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:20.580
this to give you a final fully
realized photorealistic output

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.860
such as this.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.450
So this is a single frame
here, and to run this render

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.936
on a 32-core machine
takes about an hour,

00:02:27.936 --> 00:02:30.310
just to give you a little bit
of context of what this one

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:32.490
particular rendering job is.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.970
But keep in mind the
scale of most projects.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:37.050
What I'm showing you
here is one frame.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.690
So to get one
second of animation

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.070
for a film or
television project,

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.350
you're talking about 24 frames.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.100
So right there, you're
looking at 24 hours

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.240
on a 32-core machine to render
one second of animation.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.630
And then you want to go do
10 seconds of animation,

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.370
a minute of animation,
60 minutes, 90 minutes.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:58.030
So by the time you're at a
full animated feature film,

00:02:58.030 --> 00:02:59.480
the numbers are
really astounding.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.236
And in this particular
case, it's an hour render.

00:03:02.236 --> 00:03:03.860
But in the case of
"The Good Dinosaur,"

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:05.443
this is Pixar's last
movie, I actually

00:03:05.443 --> 00:03:07.380
just saw these
stats two days ago.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:11.390
This was a 48 hour a
frame-- sorry, 48-hour

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.750
render a frame project.

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:17.090
So it was something like
175,000 frames, each one taking

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:20.710
nearly two days of render time
on a high-core-count machine.

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:23.380
So imagine when you're
delivering a film of this scale

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:24.310
and of this scope.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:26.880
You're looking at renders
in the hundreds of millions

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.600
of core hours.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:29.720
So it's very daunting.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:30.821
It's very demanding.

00:03:30.821 --> 00:03:32.820
And these numbers are
increasing year over year.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.670
Audience expectations are
getting higher and greater,

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:38.660
and the demands and the
vision of the director

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:40.750
is also increasing in scope.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.420
It's also important to note
that rendering isn't just

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:45.060
a really important
use case when you're

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.810
talking about big animated
feature films or movies

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:51.600
with superheroes or
spaceships or dinosaurs.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:52.320
It's everywhere.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.810
So rendering is
really ubiquitous

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:56.106
in what you're seeing
on television today,

00:03:56.106 --> 00:03:57.480
at the cinema, or
whatever you're

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.150
watching on your mobile device.

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:01.066
And as Monique Bradshaw
is going to talk about

00:04:01.066 --> 00:04:04.650
in greater detail, "The Walk"
is a perfect example of this.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.780
So this is a
character-driven piece.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.810
It tells the story of a
Frenchman, Philippe Petit,

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.810
who put a high wire between
the two twin towers of New York

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:13.710
and walked across it.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:15.480
So there's no spaceships here.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:16.640
There are no monsters.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.529
But in order to create
this environment,

00:04:18.529 --> 00:04:20.820
this photo-real
world to help sell

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.800
the vision of the story, the
visual effects studio Atomic

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.640
Fiction that worked on
this had to digitally

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:29.349
re-create massive parts of
New York and render this.

00:04:29.349 --> 00:04:30.890
And for a story
where there's nothing

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:32.530
obvious as a visual
effect, you're

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.020
still looking at over
9 million core hours

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:37.660
needed to make this movie.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:40.060
So it's really important to
note that any time you're

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:44.564
watching something on TV, at the
cinema, on your mobile device,

00:04:44.564 --> 00:04:46.730
it's very likely that there
was a significant amount

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.400
of rendering required
to produce that output,

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.810
whether or not you realize it.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:54.250
And so what are the challenges
associated with rendering?

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:55.910
Obviously, as
we've demonstrated,

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:58.380
it's incredibly
computationally intensive.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:04:59.930
And it's also very bursty.

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:02.720
Both Monique and Brennan
will attribute to the fact

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.590
that you're working on a project
sometimes for months at a time.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:09.641
Maybe your on-premise capacity
is at maximum utilization,

00:05:09.641 --> 00:05:11.890
and there are always last-minute
changes that come in.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:13.960
The director has new
things they want to see.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:15.307
The script's been revised.

00:05:15.307 --> 00:05:16.890
So you can never
have enough capacity,

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:19.098
especially in that month or
two before final delivery

00:05:19.098 --> 00:05:20.180
of a project.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:23.004
There's always a need for
more-- always a need for more.

00:05:23.004 --> 00:05:24.670
So bursty workloads
are really something

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:27.400
you see whether you're
working on a small commercial

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.150
and you've got five
or six projects coming

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:33.130
in at the last minute,
or a large feature film.

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:34.770
Another advantage,
or another need

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:38.920
to have centralized compute,
is in multi-site locations.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:41.870
We've really seen in the last
five to seven years studios

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:44.120
opening up worldwide locations.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:45.930
So it's not just
one studio working

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:49.617
on a project for 10 and 12
hours a day and going home,

00:05:49.617 --> 00:05:51.200
but instead they'll
work on that shot,

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:52.880
let's say at the
headquarters in London.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:54.930
That shot will get
passed off to the team

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.016
in Montreal working on it.

00:05:56.016 --> 00:05:57.640
They'll work another
eight to 10 hours.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:00.265
Then it'll get passed off to the
satellite office in Singapore.

00:06:00.265 --> 00:06:03.430
So we're seeing more and more
of a 24-hour dynamic workload,

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:05.480
even among smaller studios.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:08.600
So the need for having rendering
scalable compute resources

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:10.720
is really critical.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:13.950
And how does GCP fit
into all of this?

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:15.690
Well, our machine
capacity obviously

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:18.745
is a critical part
to all of this.

00:06:18.745 --> 00:06:21.819
GCP is the network
that runs Google.

00:06:21.819 --> 00:06:24.360
So what we serve ads and search,
and Youtube, and all of this

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.500
was built at Google scale.

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:29.240
So for us to have
a studio come to us

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.040
and need to
provision 20, 30, 40,

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:36.220
50,000 cores in a matter
of minutes is no problem.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.270
And you contrast
that with the studio

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:41.334
having to go out and
find the physical space

00:06:41.334 --> 00:06:43.750
and bring in all the machines
and set up their networking,

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:45.125
oftentimes that
they only need it

00:06:45.125 --> 00:06:46.759
for a week or two
of burst capacity.

00:06:46.759 --> 00:06:47.800
It's really not feasible.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.280
So the scalability
we offer is really

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.820
unparalleled in the industry.

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:55.050
I think our pricing
model in particular

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.750
is fantastic for bursty
rendering workloads.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:01.190
You only pay by the minute
after a 10-minute minimum.

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:03.650
So this allows you to
really scale your workload

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:04.844
in parallel.

00:07:04.844 --> 00:07:07.260
Going back to the example I
gave before of the police car,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.030
so let's say that's
one hour to render

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:11.160
and I have to render
one second of animation,

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:13.000
so that's 24 frames.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:17.300
So what I would do on GCP is
spin up 24 VMs concurrently

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:19.235
and assign one frame
to each of those.

00:07:19.235 --> 00:07:20.610
So there is no
longer the concept

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:22.840
of having to wait
for a job to queue

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:25.490
behind a limited available
number of machines.

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:27.880
But the job is done
as fast as possible,

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:29.650
and that's returned
back to your artists

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:32.780
so they can continue to iterate
and revise work in a manner

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:33.530
faster than ever.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:35.330
And when it comes
down to running

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:39.125
an animation or a visual effects
studio, on a day-to-day basis,

00:07:39.125 --> 00:07:41.500
your highest costs are really
that of your human capital.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:43.875
You're paying these artists
several hundred dollars a day

00:07:43.875 --> 00:07:44.989
to work on these shots.

00:07:44.989 --> 00:07:46.530
And if you think
about it, if they're

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:48.510
sitting there waiting
for these renders

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:51.610
to be returned while they're
in a limited on-premise

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:54.039
environment, they're not
working when they should be.

00:07:54.039 --> 00:07:55.580
They're not working
at full capacity.

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:57.205
So not only are you
taking into account

00:07:57.205 --> 00:07:59.300
the infrastructure spend,
but you're also wasting

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:01.570
a lot of this human capital.

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:03.916
And then specific to the
visual effects in animation

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:05.290
and media
entertainment industry,

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:07.349
we've done a lot
of things at GCP

00:08:07.349 --> 00:08:08.890
that really
differentiate us from any

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:11.120
of the competition out there.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:15.170
Studio-specific security is
a huge issue in our industry.

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:18.190
Often cases you're talking
about billion-dollar tentpole

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:20.140
blockbuster franchises.

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:22.807
So if any of that data
was to leak ahead of time,

00:08:22.807 --> 00:08:24.390
the Hollywood
production studios could

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:27.110
be looking at hundreds of
millions of dollars of losses.

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:29.480
So there's an incredible
amount of concern

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:31.150
over putting your
data in the cloud when

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:35.270
it comes to this sensitive,
prerelease theatrical content.

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:37.070
So what we've done at
GCP is actually work

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:38.990
directly with the Hollywood
production studios

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:42.590
educating them around our
security and our compliance.

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:44.090
And in several
cases, we've actually

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:47.280
undergone studio-specific
audits of GCP

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.130
that have awarded us a
tier I certification,

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:53.900
allowing our customers to put
the most sensitive prerelease

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:58.460
theatrical content on our
cloud for rendering workloads.

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:00.000
We've also built
scalable solutions

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.822
for all types of studios.

00:09:01.822 --> 00:09:03.280
So if you're a
small studio and you

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:05.312
want to do rendering
on GCP, we have

00:09:05.312 --> 00:09:06.520
a product called Zync Render.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:09.600
And this is a turnkey
solution, all inclusive,

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:10.550
no coding needed.

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:13.120
And it's literally a plug-in
you install inside of your host

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:17.220
application, and you can access
tens of thousands of GCP cores

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:20.340
to render your project
instantaneously.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:23.270
And for larger studios
with more custom pipelines,

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:25.882
we have a dedicated
solutions architecture guide

00:09:25.882 --> 00:09:27.590
that's been written
for us in conjunction

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:30.256
with a technical director at one
of the leading studios in North

00:09:30.256 --> 00:09:33.090
America, a studio that's been
using GCP for well over a year

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:34.580
and a half to do
their rendering.

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:36.725
And this is a full breakdown,
a step-by-step guide

00:09:36.725 --> 00:09:38.100
of how to integrate
your existing

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:40.170
on-premise infrastructure
and tie that

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:43.730
to GCP for rendering workloads.

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:45.700
And really the
backbone of all of this

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:47.880
is our ISV relationships.

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:50.420
So our industry, no matter
if you're a five-person shop

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:54.460
or you're a 1,500-person shop,
the core stock is the same.

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:57.295
Everyone is working with the
same standard commercial tools.

00:09:57.295 --> 00:09:59.170
They might have their
own additional software

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:00.670
that they've written
on top of that.

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:02.790
But it's tools like
NUKE from The Foundry,

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.980
V-Ray from ChaosGroup,
and Renderman from Pixar,

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:07.670
are the foundation
of all rendering

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:10.250
workloads in media
and entertainment.

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:13.464
So we worked very closely with
the top ISVs in the industry.

00:10:13.464 --> 00:10:14.880
And if you look
at this list here,

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:16.950
there's not a single
Oscar-winning film

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:19.380
over the last 15 years
that has not used some

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:21.460
or all of this software here.

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:23.365
So we're working very
closely with these ISVs

00:10:23.365 --> 00:10:27.180
to enable a scalable
deployment on GCP

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:28.930
that's concurrent to
the scalable model we

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:31.442
offer with our billing.

00:10:31.442 --> 00:10:33.150
And the results so
far have been awesome.

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:35.110
We've seen hundreds of
millions of core hours

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:38.810
now put through GCP
across feature film,

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:43.970
commercial advertising,
episodic television, VR content,

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:46.880
live-action cinematic rides,
and animated projects,

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:48.730
just to a name a few.

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:51.745
So we're excited to continue to
see this uptake of our product.

00:10:51.745 --> 00:10:54.020
And I'll go ahead and turn
over now, Monique Bradshaw.

00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:55.853
Again, Monique is VP
of business development

00:10:55.853 --> 00:10:57.580
and operations at
ConductorIO, and she's

00:10:57.580 --> 00:10:59.480
going to share the
story of Atomic Fiction.

00:10:59.480 --> 00:10:59.980
Monique.

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:02.440
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:05.610
MONIQUE BRADSHAW: Thanks, Todd.

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:08.350
As Todd said, I'm Monique
Bradshaw from ConductorIO,

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:12.130
and ConductorIO is a company
that provides cloud computing

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:13.040
as a service.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.840
We focus on the image-rendering
needs of the media

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.820
and entertainment space.

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:21.606
And ConductorIO provided the
cloud rendering capabilities

00:11:21.606 --> 00:11:23.480
for Atomic Fiction for
the work that they did

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:25.140
on the recent movie "The Walk."

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:27.140
As Todd said, the
movie "The Walk"

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:31.360
tells the story of-- the true
story of wire walker Philippe

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:34.570
Petit, who strung a wire,
illegally, between the two

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:37.160
towers of the World Trade
Center and walked across it

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:40.371
without any safety gear at all.

00:11:40.371 --> 00:11:41.870
And today I'm here
to share with you

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:45.610
the story of how cloud
computing played a critical role

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:49.960
in Atomic Fiction's
ability to re-create

00:11:49.960 --> 00:11:53.130
the twin towers and the
New York cityscape of 1974

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:55.150
for the movie.

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:57.725
But before I tell you
about the movie "The Walk,"

00:11:57.725 --> 00:11:59.350
I'm going to tell
you a little bit more

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:02.080
about how compute resources
are used to create

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:03.390
visual effects for film.

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:05.422
Now, Todd touched on
this a little bit,

00:12:05.422 --> 00:12:07.380
but I wanted to go in a
little bit more detail,

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:09.510
because it can get
pretty complicated.

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:11.610
So in the beginning
of a shot's life,

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:13.660
an artist works with a
lightweight wire frame

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:16.680
representation of the scene,
basically like the top image

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:17.820
there.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:20.140
Now, the relative
simplicity of this scene

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:23.860
makes it quick to interact with
locally, so a team of artists

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:25.880
can work within interactive
graphics programs

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:29.370
to create recipes
for how cameras move,

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:33.710
how surfaces are textured, and
how lighting falls on objects.

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:37.170
Now, once that recipe is
defined, it needs to be baked.

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:40.460
This process of calculating
how light is received by,

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:44.102
reflects off of millions of
surfaces is called rendering.

00:12:44.102 --> 00:12:45.560
And the results of
this process can

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:47.420
be seen in the bottom image.

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:50.840
Now, in a film, a
single second of footage

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:54.750
is typically made up
of 24 images or frames.

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:57.350
It's not unusual for a single
iteration of a single frame

00:12:57.350 --> 00:13:02.060
to take between 5 and 10
hours, sometimes even more,

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:04.660
to render on a single
multicore machine.

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:08.730
So given that, it's easy to
multiply that out and see

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:11.330
how a single iteration of
a single second of film

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:16.130
can actually require thousands
of hours of compute time.

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:18.965
So to understand how
important cloud resources were

00:13:18.965 --> 00:13:20.840
to the creation of the
effects in "The Walk,"

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:22.798
let me talk a little bit
about the hurdles that

00:13:22.798 --> 00:13:24.830
had to be overcome for this.

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:27.230
As I mentioned, ConductorIO
worked with Atomic Fiction,

00:13:27.230 --> 00:13:28.896
and they were the
visual effects company

00:13:28.896 --> 00:13:32.400
that was charged with
putting the audiences 1,400

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:36.210
feet above New York for
the climax of the film.

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:38.430
The first challenge
they had to overcome

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:40.180
was creating the environment.

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:42.180
The towers themselves had
to be built perfectly,

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:45.900
down to every last nut and bolt.
An artist then spent months

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.980
introducing imperfections to
make them look hand-built.

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:51.050
The activity in
the streets below,

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:54.190
from the cars to the people
to the flocks of birds,

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:57.450
the moving atmosphere were
all created from scratch.

00:13:57.450 --> 00:13:59.580
The scenery below
was created too,

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:03.220
with spinning fans and
rooftop HVAC systems,

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:06.920
rain gutters on buildings,
hot dog stands in the streets,

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:10.580
and changing stoplights to
make the city feel alive.

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:13.890
Every conceivable camera
angle had to be accommodated,

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:16.340
and that meant a number of
lighting scenarios-- everything

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:20.630
from nighttime to early morning
to a blustery, overcast day,

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:22.670
to a sunny fall afternoon.

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:25.180
All of that flexibility
and complexity

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:28.320
meant that a massive amount of
compute time would be required.

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:30.900
For "The Walk," a
single iteration of one

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:34.150
frame required 83
gigabytes of input data

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:37.871
and took over seven hours to
render on a 16-core instance.

00:14:37.871 --> 00:14:40.370
If you multiply that out, and
given the nature of production

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:43.430
deadlines, it meant that the
artists at Atomic Fiction

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:45.570
would need the
ability to access over

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:49.840
15,000 cores of compute
power simultaneously

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:52.700
for sustained periods of time.

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:55.000
The second challenge that
Atomic Fiction had to face

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:58.380
was the tight budget that they
had to create these effects.

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:01.070
Now, for a medium-sized
company to acquire

00:15:01.070 --> 00:15:02.870
that amount of
compute infrastructure

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:05.440
locally would require
leasing or renting

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:07.970
a large amount of
computer systems,

00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:11.860
as well as sourcing at
colocation facility,

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:14.200
the appropriate technical
support, and all of that

00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:16.158
would add up to a financial
obstacle that would

00:15:16.158 --> 00:15:17.820
have blown the budget for them.

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:20.050
Additionally, visual
effects companies

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:22.549
don't realize any profit
till the end of a project.

00:15:22.549 --> 00:15:24.590
So it was incredibly
important for Atomic Fiction

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:27.010
that they could get up and
running with as little overhead

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:27.510
as possible.

00:15:30.330 --> 00:15:31.580
This is an important graph.

00:15:31.580 --> 00:15:34.150
It illustrates the
financial challenges

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:37.500
typical of rendering needs in
the visual effects industry.

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:41.110
So as an example of a
medium-sized studio,

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:44.550
the jagged blue line represents
Atomic Fiction's actual work

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:48.770
profile and relevant spend
for compute resources.

00:15:48.770 --> 00:15:52.560
The red line represents a
typical resource profile

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:54.470
for a growing visual
effects company

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:57.570
that's investing in
on-premises resources.

00:15:57.570 --> 00:15:59.380
In this traditional
environment, resources

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:02.830
are required to try to
cater to the peak need.

00:16:02.830 --> 00:16:05.420
The time, effort,
and money required

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:08.240
to implement those
additional hardware

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:12.680
means that the resource profiles
plateau around the last peak

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:15.940
need, and they can't
adjust downward

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:19.620
to match the actual render
workload at given times.

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:22.440
So what you can see here is
that the pink shaded area

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:25.960
is where on-site resources
are overprovisioned

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:28.780
for the actual
workload, and that

00:16:28.780 --> 00:16:31.240
represents wasted investment.

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:34.740
The blue shaded area is
where on-site resources were

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:37.330
underprovisioned for
workload requirements,

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:40.890
and that represents a negative
impact to artist productivity.

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:43.400
So as you can see,
the spiky nature

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:48.410
of typical content creation
workload results in companies

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:51.070
cycling between the cost
of limited computing

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:54.500
power negatively impacting the
productivity of their artists

00:16:54.500 --> 00:17:00.170
and too much deadweight being
carried during downtimes.

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:04.040
So the solution to these
challenges of complexity,

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:07.170
cost, and challenging
work profiles

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:09.510
was to utilize the flexible
capacity of the cloud.

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:12.210
And that's where
ConductorIO comes in.

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:14.980
So Atomic Fiction utilized
Conductor's cloud rendering

00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:19.190
platform to manage its cloud
computing needs for the film.

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:21.210
Now, the benefits
of cloud computing

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:24.359
can really be summed
up in one word-- scale.

00:17:24.359 --> 00:17:26.700
If you take a look
at this simple table,

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:28.190
you can get a very
clear contrast

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:31.170
in two styles of managing work.

00:17:31.170 --> 00:17:33.720
In the first column,
you see 1,000 tasks

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:37.000
spread across 50 instances that
result in a total turnaround

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:38.900
time of 10 hours.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:40.850
In the second column,
you see the same number

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:43.800
of tasks spread across
1,000 instances,

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:48.000
resulting in a turnaround
time of only half an hour.

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:49.936
Now, as you can see,
there are good reasons

00:17:49.936 --> 00:17:51.560
for each example of
resource allowance,

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:54.360
but one of the biggest
benefits of elastic scaling

00:17:54.360 --> 00:17:56.900
is the ability to scale up
rapidly as the need demands,

00:17:56.900 --> 00:17:59.310
allowing artists
to iterate faster.

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:01.911
That improves productivity
and creative quality

00:18:01.911 --> 00:18:03.660
by limiting the amount
of time the artists

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:06.070
have to wait around to see
the outcome of their work.

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:09.260
And it allows them to be
creative and iterate faster

00:18:09.260 --> 00:18:11.920
in the same amount
of calendar time.

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:14.380
Large peak capacity
and rapid downscaling

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:17.560
lets teams postpone
their render needs

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:20.520
until later in the schedule,
and that's creatively

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.880
and financially beneficial.

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:25.760
The on-demand scalability
afforded by GCP

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:28.850
allows companies to maintain
a direct relationship

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:32.010
between project
cost and revenue.

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:34.070
No longer does the
studio's revenue

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:37.790
need to cover unused but
costly infrastructure.

00:18:37.790 --> 00:18:41.040
In addition, these
days, data and analytics

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:45.270
are critical aspects of
pretty much any company.

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:47.730
The scale of data
collection and analysis

00:18:47.730 --> 00:18:49.410
of large amounts
of metadata that's

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:51.860
enabled by leveraging
cloud architecture

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:53.740
also allows for
better cost management

00:18:53.740 --> 00:18:57.190
and streamlining of workflows.

00:18:57.190 --> 00:19:01.459
So Conductor's written on top
of the Google Cloud platform,

00:19:01.459 --> 00:19:03.500
and it provides functionality
critical to meeting

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:06.900
the needs of a demanding
visual effects production.

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:09.320
For us, there were a
number of key factors

00:19:09.320 --> 00:19:13.060
that we needed to evaluate
in choosing to use Google.

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:15.350
The big one from a
business perspective

00:19:15.350 --> 00:19:19.800
is the ability to do permanent
billing, which Google enables.

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:22.830
When we're providing a service
to an industry where workloads

00:19:22.830 --> 00:19:26.360
are often broken down into tasks
that require fractions of hours

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:31.630
of compute time, the ability
to offer true pay per use

00:19:31.630 --> 00:19:33.650
is a critical aspect
and a critical part

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:36.750
of our value proposition.

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:39.560
Now, obviously Google's
a global company,

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:43.240
and that global presence is
incredibly important for us.

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:46.030
Single customers that we have
may have studios in locations

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:49.490
as diverse as Europe,
North America, and India,

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:52.740
and being able to provide a
standardized service that they

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:54.960
can interact with from any
location in the same way

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:57.380
is very important.

00:19:57.380 --> 00:20:01.120
Also, having access to the vast
engineering talent of Google

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:03.720
to help us work through
architecture optimization

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:05.940
or resolve issues
gives us the confidence

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:09.220
to provide a service to
businesses where up time is

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:12.120
incredibly important.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:15.160
Now, one of the holy grails
for the visual effects industry

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:16.900
is really being
able to mine data

00:20:16.900 --> 00:20:21.559
for bidding accuracy and
thorough render cost analysis.

00:20:21.559 --> 00:20:23.350
And accurately predicting
the time and cost

00:20:23.350 --> 00:20:25.869
for a given render
job is also critical.

00:20:25.869 --> 00:20:28.410
Now, to do that at scale, where
we have thousands or millions

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:32.710
of concurrent tasks
generating data constantly,

00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:34.460
we needed a powerful
database option.

00:20:34.460 --> 00:20:35.626
And for us, that's Bigtable.

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:41.900
With Bigtable, it's
huge, it's extensible,

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:44.180
and it's incredibly responsive.

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:47.280
And when we have huge numbers
of jobs running at the same time

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:49.930
through Conductor,
we can then use

00:20:49.930 --> 00:20:52.674
BigQuery to access that
data and present it quickly

00:20:52.674 --> 00:20:55.340
to our customers, providing them
real-time information about how

00:20:55.340 --> 00:20:57.280
their work is going.

00:20:57.280 --> 00:20:59.760
Finally, Google App
Engine offers us

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:01.749
a ton of configurability
out of the box, which

00:21:01.749 --> 00:21:03.790
is really important for
a small company to get up

00:21:03.790 --> 00:21:05.384
and running quickly.

00:21:05.384 --> 00:21:07.300
And it's an important
capability for our team,

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:11.610
because it allows us to focus
on implementing our key roadmap

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:14.200
features rather than worrying
about our application

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:16.760
architecture.

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:19.240
All right, so to talk a
little bit about conductor

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:23.050
architecture, one of the
most important things for us

00:21:23.050 --> 00:21:25.450
was to have as little impact
on the artist's workflow as

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:26.890
possible.

00:21:26.890 --> 00:21:31.810
So we created plug-ins that
fit into the most commonly

00:21:31.810 --> 00:21:34.410
used software for
artists, and then

00:21:34.410 --> 00:21:36.862
that is installed in the
artist's local machine.

00:21:36.862 --> 00:21:38.445
Other than that, the
artist's workflow

00:21:38.445 --> 00:21:40.340
is pretty much unchanged.

00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:41.590
They work as they always have.

00:21:41.590 --> 00:21:43.214
They save their data
to the right place

00:21:43.214 --> 00:21:45.860
in the local network,
and that's it.

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:47.470
When the artist is
ready to render,

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:50.500
they pull up the installed
conductor plug-in,

00:21:50.500 --> 00:21:53.280
enter a few bits of
parameters, things

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:56.570
like choosing the instance type,
setting up the frame range,

00:21:56.570 --> 00:21:58.970
and then they just hit Submit.

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:01.050
The plug-in then sends
those job parameters

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:02.760
to the Conductor
application, which

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:05.270
serves as the brain of
the Conductor service.

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:07.770
The application is hosted
in Google App Engine,

00:22:07.770 --> 00:22:12.390
and there it initiates a
number of key functions.

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:16.510
So the app uploads
dependent files and data,

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:20.010
including files, scripts,
and environment variables

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:22.740
via an uploader daemon
from the local file system

00:22:22.740 --> 00:22:26.130
to the cloud, where we also do
a deduplication process to make

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:27.990
sure that we're
using the storage as

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:30.030
effectively as possible.

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:32.750
The data is initially
stored in GCS,

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:36.910
and then specific instances are
spun up to sync data from GCS

00:22:36.910 --> 00:22:39.620
to a Gluster and a [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:39.620 --> 00:22:46.290
And we scale up at 100 render
instances per cluster instance

00:22:46.290 --> 00:22:49.580
in order to meet our IO needs.

00:22:49.580 --> 00:22:52.890
Now, the Conductor app also
determines the required number

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:54.980
of render instances and
spins up the right number

00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:58.560
from Google Compute Engine,
scaling up quickly, reliably,

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:00.720
and massively.

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:03.930
We run a Docker container that's
customized for our execution

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:04.870
environment.

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:08.780
Using Docker allows us
to potentially accept

00:23:08.780 --> 00:23:12.270
custom images from customers,
and that's a major benefit

00:23:12.270 --> 00:23:14.790
for highly customized studios.

00:23:14.790 --> 00:23:17.490
The render instances
read the necessary input

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:20.340
files from the Gluster share.

00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:22.150
Once all the
components are running

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:24.890
and active, the Google
app-- or excuse me,

00:23:24.890 --> 00:23:26.940
the Conductor app manages
the rendering process

00:23:26.940 --> 00:23:30.190
from start to finish,
including licensing, queueing,

00:23:30.190 --> 00:23:33.900
error handling, and
metrics collection.

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:36.450
The web UI, also hosted
from Google App Engine,

00:23:36.450 --> 00:23:38.810
allows users to control
and monitor the render jobs

00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:39.820
from wherever they are.

00:23:42.550 --> 00:23:46.660
Datastore is used
to store the output,

00:23:46.660 --> 00:23:48.370
to store state and operations.

00:23:48.370 --> 00:23:51.610
Job metrics are stored in
BigQuery for easy access,

00:23:51.610 --> 00:23:54.409
and also made available
via the web dashboards.

00:23:54.409 --> 00:23:57.578
Once a job completes, the output
is stored in GCS and Gluster

00:23:57.578 --> 00:24:00.330
NFS for future ease of use.

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:02.883
The data is also downloaded
to a local path specified

00:24:02.883 --> 00:24:04.257
at job submission,
so the artists

00:24:04.257 --> 00:24:06.880
have that in their
local environment again.

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:09.600
If there are no further jobs
requiring that same instance

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:11.410
configuration, then
the Conductor app

00:24:11.410 --> 00:24:13.098
shuts down any
unneeded instances.

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:19.720
So what does that
really mean and result

00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:21.050
in when it's put into use?

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:23.390
Let's take a quick look
at a quick two-minute clip

00:24:23.390 --> 00:24:25.750
of some of the effects that
were for "The Walk" that

00:24:25.750 --> 00:24:27.864
were generated using Conductor.

00:24:27.864 --> 00:24:30.230
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:24:30.230 --> 00:24:33.218
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:26:41.343 --> 00:26:41.926
[END PLAYBACK]

00:26:41.926 --> 00:26:43.918
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:43.918 --> 00:26:48.010
So that gives you an idea
of the real complexity that

00:26:48.010 --> 00:26:51.252
goes into making a
movie, and also it

00:26:51.252 --> 00:26:53.460
tells you that the best
effects are the ones that you

00:26:53.460 --> 00:26:55.260
don't even know are there.

00:26:55.260 --> 00:26:58.030
So here's the final breakdown
for the cloud computing

00:26:58.030 --> 00:27:00.320
that went into
creating those effects.

00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:03.140
Over 9 million core
hours were used

00:27:03.140 --> 00:27:05.410
to create about 30
minutes of film.

00:27:05.410 --> 00:27:08.920
The final portion of the film
was all done through Conductor.

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:13.708
And of those 9 million hours,
almost 3 million of them

00:27:13.708 --> 00:27:15.160
were in the last month alone.

00:27:15.160 --> 00:27:18.700
It gives you amazing evidence
of the power and flexibility

00:27:18.700 --> 00:27:20.690
of scalability.

00:27:20.690 --> 00:27:22.440
Now, all of that
equated to a peak total

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:25.115
of over 15,000
concurrent cores running

00:27:25.115 --> 00:27:29.820
in Google Cloud for
sustained periods of time.

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:31.880
Another major benefit of
the distributed nature

00:27:31.880 --> 00:27:34.046
of cloud computing is how
accessible it is to people

00:27:34.046 --> 00:27:34.982
all over the world.

00:27:34.982 --> 00:27:37.940
For the portions of this movie
worked on by Atomic Fiction,

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:41.230
75 artists collaborated from two
different locations-- the Bay

00:27:41.230 --> 00:27:44.190
Area and Montreal,
Canada-- without any change

00:27:44.190 --> 00:27:47.280
to workflow or additional
management overhead.

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:50.067
As you can see, in order to
accommodate the peak render

00:27:50.067 --> 00:27:52.400
load, we estimate that it
would have cost Atomic Fiction

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:56.350
about $1.5 million to
procure the necessary local

00:27:56.350 --> 00:27:57.780
infrastructure.

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:00.680
By using Conductor and the
power of cloud computing,

00:28:00.680 --> 00:28:04.620
Atomic Fiction realized a cost
of about 50% just on render

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:07.434
infrastructure alone.

00:28:07.434 --> 00:28:08.850
Now, as impressive
as all of those

00:28:08.850 --> 00:28:11.890
numbers are, for me,
this quote really

00:28:11.890 --> 00:28:16.490
captures the power and the
promise of cloud architecture.

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:18.970
This is what we have
the ability to do

00:28:18.970 --> 00:28:20.920
when we are able to
leverage the capabilities

00:28:20.920 --> 00:28:22.560
and flexibility of the cloud.

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:24.770
We have the ability to
fundamentally change

00:28:24.770 --> 00:28:26.930
the way an industry works.

00:28:26.930 --> 00:28:28.230
Thank you very much.

00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:29.920
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:32.985 --> 00:28:35.360
TODD PRIVES: Thanks for that,
Monique-- some really great

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:37.440
insight into how they
built their platform using

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:39.650
GCP and some of the
amazing photo-real

00:28:39.650 --> 00:28:41.540
visual effects work
that they completed.

00:28:41.540 --> 00:28:43.748
So now we hear from Brennan
Chapman-- Brennan, again,

00:28:43.748 --> 00:28:46.230
the head of pipeline at
Oscar-winning Moonbot Studios.

00:28:46.230 --> 00:28:47.480
BRENNAN CHAPMAN: Thanks, Todd.

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:48.690
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:49.650 --> 00:28:53.940
Hi, I'm Brennan Chapman, head
of pipeline at Moonbot Studios.

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:56.000
Moonbot is based out of
Shreveport, Louisiana.

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:58.020
We have about 50
people on staff.

00:28:58.020 --> 00:29:00.710
We've been honored with
quite a few different awards.

00:29:00.710 --> 00:29:02.710
A couple of projects you
might recognize us from

00:29:02.710 --> 00:29:04.168
include "The
Fantastic Flying Books

00:29:04.168 --> 00:29:06.620
of Mr. Morris Lessmore,"
"The Scarecrow,"

00:29:06.620 --> 00:29:07.790
as well as "The Numberlys."

00:29:07.790 --> 00:29:11.110
These take multiple forms,
including short films, apps,

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:13.260
games, and even printed books.

00:29:13.260 --> 00:29:15.860
We pride ourselves as being
a multi-platform storytelling

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:18.050
studio, and we use
whatever medium

00:29:18.050 --> 00:29:21.204
that we see best fit
to tell our stories.

00:29:21.204 --> 00:29:22.870
A story I'd like to
