00:30:12.187 --> 00:30:12.770
[END PLAYBACK]

00:30:12.770 --> 00:30:13.260
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:15.093
BRENNAN CHAPMAN: Hope
you guys enjoyed that.

00:30:17.710 --> 00:30:21.640
So that story was great, and we
used a lot of cloud rendering

00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:22.140
on it.

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:24.710
But the question is, why use
cloud rendering at Moonbot?

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:26.610
What is the benefit we get?

00:30:26.610 --> 00:30:28.500
And we've sort of touched
on it a little bit,

00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:30.958
but just to state it from an
exec at Moonbot's perspective,

00:30:30.958 --> 00:30:32.485
it increases our
creative freedom,

00:30:32.485 --> 00:30:34.110
which is our golden
ticket for Moonbot.

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:36.890
It allows our artists
to be more creative.

00:30:36.890 --> 00:30:38.440
It limits them
from the restraints

00:30:38.440 --> 00:30:41.570
of the fixed capacity
of a local render farm,

00:30:41.570 --> 00:30:46.170
to be able to scale and
accommodate any creative goal.

00:30:46.170 --> 00:30:49.240
And thus because of that, we're
able to meet our deadlines

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:51.170
and not go past
them, which is huge.

00:30:51.170 --> 00:30:53.200
And also, it helps
us to save money.

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:57.270
As they've stated, the rendering
is a lot of burst workflow.

00:30:57.270 --> 00:31:00.020
And if we don't have to buy a
local compute farm that scales

00:31:00.020 --> 00:31:02.800
to the peak times
and we underutilize

00:31:02.800 --> 00:31:06.880
the capacity at other times,
we save a lot of money.

00:31:06.880 --> 00:31:09.415
Similar to Monique's graph,
here's a graph of-- last year

00:31:09.415 --> 00:31:11.540
we had about three projects
that we were rendering.

00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:13.940
They were all sort of
going on at the same time,

00:31:13.940 --> 00:31:17.240
including this project we just
showed you, "Taking Flight."

00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:19.334
And you can see three main
things with this graph.

00:31:19.334 --> 00:31:21.500
One, you can see that there's
these high peak times.

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:24.230
There's times when we have
enormous amount of renders.

00:31:24.230 --> 00:31:26.270
And by the way, this is
a graph of the amount

00:31:26.270 --> 00:31:30.229
of hours each artists have
submitted per day to the farm.

00:31:30.229 --> 00:31:31.770
The second thing
you can see is there

00:31:31.770 --> 00:31:34.762
are times where we hardly use
anything, maybe none at all.

00:31:34.762 --> 00:31:36.220
And the third thing
that you'll see

00:31:36.220 --> 00:31:38.690
is there's this orange line that
we sort of target at Moonbot,

00:31:38.690 --> 00:31:40.700
and that's the sort of
medium, the stuff that we

00:31:40.700 --> 00:31:41.860
use all the time.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:45.320
And our goal is to sort of
have local compute capacity

00:31:45.320 --> 00:31:46.780
for that median line.

00:31:46.780 --> 00:31:48.995
Anything above that, we want
to push it to the cloud,

00:31:48.995 --> 00:31:50.910
'cause that's more
cost effective for us.

00:31:50.910 --> 00:31:54.900
But anything below that, we'll
render locally at Moonbot.

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:57.110
So our rendering goals for
implementation at Moonbot

00:31:57.110 --> 00:32:00.605
was we wanted it to be easy to
use, we wanted local and cloud,

00:32:00.605 --> 00:32:03.040
so we wanted a hybrid
approach, and we only

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:04.550
wanted to use cloud when needed.

00:32:04.550 --> 00:32:05.870
We didn't want to
treat cloud the way

00:32:05.870 --> 00:32:07.290
we do local nodes,
where we basically just

00:32:07.290 --> 00:32:09.600
spit out 50 nodes that just
sit there in the cloud.

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:12.500
We wanted to only spin up
nodes when we're using them,

00:32:12.500 --> 00:32:15.710
and we try to be smart
about how that works.

00:32:15.710 --> 00:32:18.310
So there's two render farm
strategies that we see.

00:32:18.310 --> 00:32:19.910
If we do everything
on the cloud,

00:32:19.910 --> 00:32:21.480
you get sort of the
render everything

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:23.380
as soon as possible
strategy, and that's

00:32:23.380 --> 00:32:26.820
because if you rent
one node for 100 hours,

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:28.620
you're paying the
same cost on the cloud

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:31.200
as renting 100
nodes for one hour.

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:33.992
So if it costs the same,
why not render it faster?

00:32:33.992 --> 00:32:34.659
So that's great.

00:32:34.659 --> 00:32:35.533
So that's cloud only.

00:32:35.533 --> 00:32:38.260
But with a hybrid farm, you sort
of get a different approach.

00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:39.930
You have this local
compute capacity,

00:32:39.930 --> 00:32:42.570
and you get the best cost
and savings when you fully

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:44.420
utilize the local
compute capacity,

00:32:44.420 --> 00:32:46.642
and anything extra
you push to the cloud.

00:32:46.642 --> 00:32:48.350
But with that, you
need a time component.

00:32:48.350 --> 00:32:50.516
You need sort of a deadline
for each of your renders

00:32:50.516 --> 00:32:53.230
to be able to calculate, am
I fully using my local farm?

00:32:53.230 --> 00:32:56.080
And once that's done,
you push it to the cloud.

00:32:56.080 --> 00:32:59.090
So to do that, we present
our artist with this dialog

00:32:59.090 --> 00:33:00.600
when they submit
stuff to the farm.

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:03.360
And the first option just asks
them, when do you need this by?

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:05.360
And they can set whatever
time factor they want.

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:07.160
It defaults to the next
morning, because a lot

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:09.120
of times they submit before
they leave for the day.

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:10.494
But this gives us
our time factor

00:33:10.494 --> 00:33:13.229
to be able to know, when is
a render being completed by?

00:33:13.229 --> 00:33:15.520
And if we have time, let's
render it on the local farm.

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:19.340
And if not, we'll
render it in the cloud.

00:33:19.340 --> 00:33:19.840
So great.

00:33:19.840 --> 00:33:22.410
So why did we choose
Google Cloud to do this?

00:33:22.410 --> 00:33:23.160
Three main things.

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:24.210
They had great support.

00:33:24.210 --> 00:33:26.280
So talking with them
originally, they've

00:33:26.280 --> 00:33:28.250
helped us [? fix ?]
a lot of stuff,

00:33:28.250 --> 00:33:31.420
and set up our pipeline
to easily support this.

00:33:31.420 --> 00:33:34.140
They even suggested the
second option, Avere,

00:33:34.140 --> 00:33:36.910
as our storage vendor, and I'll
get into that in one minute.

00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:39.370
And also they have permanent
billing, which is huge for us.

00:33:39.370 --> 00:33:41.370
We only pay for pretty
much exactly what we use.

00:33:41.370 --> 00:33:43.340
We're not paying
for our time slots

00:33:43.340 --> 00:33:46.920
that we might not use
the full capacity of.

00:33:46.920 --> 00:33:49.206
So implementing the cloud
rendering into our pipeline,

00:33:49.206 --> 00:33:50.830
we sort of faced
three main challenges,

00:33:50.830 --> 00:33:53.880
and I'd like to share with
you some details with those.

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:56.880
First is storage-- how do we
transfer our files to the cloud

00:33:56.880 --> 00:33:58.882
to be able to render with them?

00:33:58.882 --> 00:34:00.340
So here's what we
need to transfer.

00:34:00.340 --> 00:34:02.700
We need to transfer 3d
scene files and textures,

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:04.810
and then we need to
download the rendered frames

00:34:04.810 --> 00:34:06.919
and maybe logs back from them.

00:34:06.919 --> 00:34:07.960
We're also in Shreveport.

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:09.659
We don't have the fastest
internet available.

00:34:09.659 --> 00:34:11.100
We're sort of limited
to this 100-megabit

00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:11.880
per second connection.

00:34:11.880 --> 00:34:14.254
So we have to have to be smart
about what we're uploading

00:34:14.254 --> 00:34:15.495
and when we're uploading it.

00:34:15.495 --> 00:34:17.870
There's two ways that we saw
that we could sort of attack

00:34:17.870 --> 00:34:18.489
this problem.

00:34:18.489 --> 00:34:20.960
The first way is by writing
sort of custom scripts targeted

00:34:20.960 --> 00:34:22.440
into each application.

00:34:22.440 --> 00:34:24.832
So how does the application
load in data, and we

00:34:24.832 --> 00:34:27.040
have to isolate all that,
maybe parse the scene files

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:28.280
or something like that.

00:34:28.280 --> 00:34:29.370
And then whenever
you render it out,

00:34:29.370 --> 00:34:31.161
we have to target and
find what it rendered

00:34:31.161 --> 00:34:33.500
and transfer those back locally.

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:36.870
But this is done very
individual application specific.

00:34:36.870 --> 00:34:38.790
So we also, through
Google's help,

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:41.219
we figured out there's
another solution called Avere,

00:34:41.219 --> 00:34:44.070
and this solution acts as like
a cache to your file system.

00:34:44.070 --> 00:34:46.070
And because it happens
at the file system level,

00:34:46.070 --> 00:34:48.028
It can be sort of a
one-size-fits-all approach.

00:34:48.028 --> 00:34:50.330
So let's look more at that.

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:52.980
So here's sort of a graph of how
our infrastructure is set up.

00:34:52.980 --> 00:34:55.438
We have an Avere cache node
that sort of sits in the cloud,

00:34:55.438 --> 00:34:57.660
and we have our core
filer that sits on site.

00:34:57.660 --> 00:34:59.560
And Avere will
automatically upload data

00:34:59.560 --> 00:35:01.930
as it's requested to the cloud.

00:35:01.930 --> 00:35:04.681
And the cloud keeps this cache
that can be then distributed

00:35:04.681 --> 00:35:05.680
to all the render nodes.

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:08.220
The render nodes connect into
Avere and just ask for stuff.

00:35:08.220 --> 00:35:10.470
It doesn't have it, it uploads
it from the core filer.

00:35:10.470 --> 00:35:14.310
If it already has it, it just
serves it out of the cache.

00:35:14.310 --> 00:35:16.720
The way this works is
it mounts via the NFS,

00:35:16.720 --> 00:35:19.370
which is the same way our
local storage handles,

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:21.180
which creates this
nice uniformity

00:35:21.180 --> 00:35:25.050
between our local cloud
and our Google Cloud.

00:35:25.050 --> 00:35:26.330
Makes it nice and easy.

00:35:26.330 --> 00:35:28.950
It also autouploads dependent
files, since you're doing this

00:35:28.950 --> 00:35:31.870
just at a file system
level, and also supports

00:35:31.870 --> 00:35:33.020
partial file transfer.

00:35:33.020 --> 00:35:35.125
So if you only need the
top of head of your file,

00:35:35.125 --> 00:35:36.750
you don't need to
read the whole thing.

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:40.940
Avere is smart enough to only
transfer that part of the file.

00:35:40.940 --> 00:35:43.190
The other challenge is, how
do we set up render nodes?

00:35:43.190 --> 00:35:45.580
How do we preferably
have a uniformity

00:35:45.580 --> 00:35:48.285
between how we have local nodes
set up and the cloud nodes?

00:35:48.285 --> 00:35:51.170
And we came up
with this workflow.

00:35:51.170 --> 00:35:52.530
We take sort of a base OS.

00:35:52.530 --> 00:35:54.686
We use CentOS in our
case in the cloud.

00:35:54.686 --> 00:35:56.560
And we run our configuration
management tool.

00:35:56.560 --> 00:35:59.650
We use Ansible at Moonbot to
configure our local nodes.

00:35:59.650 --> 00:36:01.820
And then we can just run
that the same thing again

00:36:01.820 --> 00:36:03.609
in Google Cloud on
the base OS to get

00:36:03.609 --> 00:36:05.650
all the software and
configuration and everything

00:36:05.650 --> 00:36:07.300
installed in a VM.

00:36:07.300 --> 00:36:09.404
We then bake that to an
imaging in Google Cloud,

00:36:09.404 --> 00:36:11.570
plug it into an instance
template and instance group

00:36:11.570 --> 00:36:15.190
to be able to spawn
pools of those nodes.

00:36:15.190 --> 00:36:17.025
There's two types
of instance types

00:36:17.025 --> 00:36:18.150
that we look at at Moonbot.

00:36:18.150 --> 00:36:20.732
There's the on-demand
and preemptible nodes.

00:36:20.732 --> 00:36:22.690
Our goal at Moonbot is
to try to take advantage

00:36:22.690 --> 00:36:24.398
of the preemptible
nodes, because they're

00:36:24.398 --> 00:36:25.600
less than half the cost.

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:27.099
But they come with
two side effects.

00:36:27.099 --> 00:36:28.710
Their availability
is not guaranteed,

00:36:28.710 --> 00:36:31.824
and they also refresh
after every 24 hours.

00:36:31.824 --> 00:36:33.240
The benefit, though,
is the render

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:36.600
farms are capable of
handling fault tolerances,

00:36:36.600 --> 00:36:39.670
like if a frame fails, it
will automatically be retried.

00:36:39.670 --> 00:36:42.710
So thus, by nature, as long
as the preemptible instances

00:36:42.710 --> 00:36:45.160
aren't too wildly
unavailable, we

00:36:45.160 --> 00:36:47.180
can take advantage of
this preemptible instances

00:36:47.180 --> 00:36:50.490
and take advantage
of the cost savings.

00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:52.990
And we use instance groups to
manage the pools of instances.

00:36:52.990 --> 00:36:55.920
This is especially useful when
you have preemptible instances,

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:57.290
because their availability
is not guaranteed

00:36:57.290 --> 00:36:58.650
and sometimes they'll go away.

00:36:58.650 --> 00:37:00.233
The instance groups
will automatically

00:37:00.233 --> 00:37:01.860
manage adding them
back if they ever

00:37:01.860 --> 00:37:04.120
become available again,
so we don't have to write

00:37:04.120 --> 00:37:05.720
all the code to handle that.

00:37:05.720 --> 00:37:08.300
We're also able to start and
stop these pools of instances,

00:37:08.300 --> 00:37:11.077
and we sort of see a start-up
time about three minutes

00:37:11.077 --> 00:37:12.160
to get everything running.

00:37:12.160 --> 00:37:13.659
So I can go into
the instance group,

00:37:13.659 --> 00:37:15.690
say I need 50
nodes or 100 nodes,

00:37:15.690 --> 00:37:17.740
and three minutes later
they're on my farm

00:37:17.740 --> 00:37:21.400
rendering and doing the work.

00:37:21.400 --> 00:37:23.170
Instance performance
was interesting, too.

00:37:23.170 --> 00:37:25.989
We found that not all instances
in Google Cloud are the same.

00:37:25.989 --> 00:37:28.030
Be careful if you're doing
this, because we found

00:37:28.030 --> 00:37:30.060
that Haswell nodes
were about 20%

00:37:30.060 --> 00:37:31.480
faster than the
Ivy Bridge nodes,

00:37:31.480 --> 00:37:33.479
even though they might
have a lower clock speed,

00:37:33.479 --> 00:37:35.740
for our specific
rendering applications.

00:37:35.740 --> 00:37:38.310
And with that, we sort of
saw an 80% performance match

00:37:38.310 --> 00:37:40.550
with cloud nodes
compared to local nodes.

00:37:40.550 --> 00:37:42.030
So it is slower,
but the benefit is

00:37:42.030 --> 00:37:45.430
we can have a whole
lot more of them.

00:37:45.430 --> 00:37:47.270
So the last challenge
we really faced

00:37:47.270 --> 00:37:48.930
is how to forecast renders.

00:37:48.930 --> 00:37:50.840
So if we want to do
this hybrid approach,

00:37:50.840 --> 00:37:52.940
how do we really get that
data to be able to know

00:37:52.940 --> 00:37:55.611
when to submit to the cloud?

00:37:55.611 --> 00:37:57.235
So what we need to
compute is some type

00:37:57.235 --> 00:37:59.557
of total number of the
pending render hours,

00:37:59.557 --> 00:38:01.640
and then from that maybe
we get the delayed render

00:38:01.640 --> 00:38:03.996
hours at sort of a
specific point in time.

00:38:03.996 --> 00:38:05.970
So the way that we
do this is an artist

00:38:05.970 --> 00:38:07.970
submits their job to the
farm, using the dialog

00:38:07.970 --> 00:38:09.900
like you saw earlier,
and we split it off

00:38:09.900 --> 00:38:11.110
into two main groups.

00:38:11.110 --> 00:38:13.700
We have sort of preview
frames and other frames.

00:38:13.700 --> 00:38:16.160
So what that means is you
might have a shot that's

00:38:16.160 --> 00:38:17.255
about 1,000 frames long.

00:38:17.255 --> 00:38:18.880
You would take the
first, maybe middle,

00:38:18.880 --> 00:38:21.463
and last, and you would render
those with the highest priority

00:38:21.463 --> 00:38:23.460
first, maybe on your
local farm-- just whatever

00:38:23.460 --> 00:38:24.980
gets it done fastest.

00:38:24.980 --> 00:38:28.057
Then you take the time that
it took to render those frames

00:38:28.057 --> 00:38:29.640
and use that to
estimate the time it's

00:38:29.640 --> 00:38:31.700
going to take to render
all of the other frames.

00:38:31.700 --> 00:38:34.420
We do this for each of the jobs
that are submitted to our farm,

00:38:34.420 --> 00:38:38.740
and we end up with a total count
of the pending render hours.

00:38:38.740 --> 00:38:41.020
We then use benchmarks
of our local nodes

00:38:41.020 --> 00:38:43.240
to be able to see how
much local capacity

00:38:43.240 --> 00:38:44.960
we'll have in a
certain amount of time.

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:47.850
And then we can get a benchmark
of how many delayed hours there

00:38:47.850 --> 00:38:48.904
are in the cloud.

00:38:48.904 --> 00:38:50.570
We can use benchmarks
of our cloud nodes

00:38:50.570 --> 00:38:52.820
to then get an exact count
of how many cloud nodes

00:38:52.820 --> 00:38:55.990
we'll need to add for
any time and to complete

00:38:55.990 --> 00:38:59.360
our renders by their deadlines.

00:38:59.360 --> 00:38:59.860
So great.

00:38:59.860 --> 00:39:03.025
So that's most of what we do
at Moonbot to get everything

00:39:03.025 --> 00:39:04.400
on cloud rendering,
and we end up

00:39:04.400 --> 00:39:05.830
with these three main benefits.

00:39:05.830 --> 00:39:07.140
We get creative freedom.

00:39:07.140 --> 00:39:08.020
We're more flexible.

00:39:08.020 --> 00:39:09.495
We can scale on demand.

00:39:09.495 --> 00:39:11.670
It removes constraints on
scheduling, which is huge.

00:39:11.670 --> 00:39:13.170
I can't tell you
how many times I've

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:15.530
had to rebudget based
on schedules shifting.

00:39:15.530 --> 00:39:19.150
But the cloud enables that
to be much more flexible.

00:39:19.150 --> 00:39:19.960
It saves time.

00:39:19.960 --> 00:39:22.850
We get faster rendering,
simplified budget planning,

00:39:22.850 --> 00:39:24.530
and increased flexibility.

00:39:24.530 --> 00:39:25.360
We pay as we go.

00:39:25.360 --> 00:39:27.740
We don't have to have this
capital expenditure up front.

00:39:27.740 --> 00:39:31.330
And it enabled us to succeed
as a company at Moonbot.

00:39:31.330 --> 00:39:33.626
So thank you.

00:39:33.626 --> 00:39:34.420
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:34.420 --> 00:39:37.120
And finish it up with Todd.

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:39.292
TODD PRIVES: Thank you.

00:39:39.292 --> 00:39:41.000
Thanks everyone for
coming to our session

00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:42.708
and hearing from our
two great presenters

00:39:42.708 --> 00:39:45.750
on how GCP has really enabled
them to dynamically scale

00:39:45.750 --> 00:39:47.340
their businesses
in a manner that's

00:39:47.340 --> 00:39:49.360
cost effective and
really, most importantly,

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:51.030
gives them a lot of
creative freedom.

00:39:51.030 --> 00:39:52.480
So thanks again.

00:39:52.480 --> 00:39:55.610
And we'll see you all tonight
at GCP after next-- after dark.

00:39:55.610 --> 00:39:56.210
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:56.210 --> 00:39:58.360
